Because a product can appear in several tabs, the Hero tag can't be just the product ID. Tabs built with `IndexedStack`, `AutomaticKeepAliveClientMixin` or `PageStorage` keep every tab's grid alive in the same route. Two Heroes with the same tag then trigger "There are multiple heroes that share the same tag within a subtree", or the flight picks the wrong source.

The fix is to make the tag unique per product and per origin (tab), and pass that exact tag to the detail screen.

## 1. Build the tag in one place

```dart
class HeroTags {
  static Object product(String tabId, String productId) =>
      'product-image/$tabId/$productId';
}
```

## 2. Grid item (source)

```dart
class ProductGridItem extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProductGridItem({
    super.key,
    required this.product,
    required this.tabId,
  });

  final Product product;
  final String tabId;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    final heroTag = HeroTags.product(tabId, product.id);

    return GestureDetector(
      onTap: () => Navigator.of(context).push(
        MaterialPageRoute(
          builder: (_) => ProductDetailScreen(
            product: product,
            heroTag: heroTag,
          ),
        ),
      ),
      child: Hero(
        tag: heroTag,
        child: Image.network(product.imageUrl, fit: BoxFit.cover),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## 3. Detail screen (destination)

```dart
class ProductDetailScreen extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProductDetailScreen({
    super.key,
    required this.product,
    required this.heroTag,
  });

  final Product product;
  final Object heroTag;

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(title: Text(product.name)),
      body: Column(
        children: [
          Hero(
            tag: heroTag,
            child: Image.network(product.imageUrl,
                width: double.infinity, height: 300, fit: BoxFit.cover),
          ),
          // ...rest of details
        ],
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

## 4. Pass `tabId` from each tab

```dart
ProductGridItem(product: p, tabId: 'favorites')
ProductGridItem(product: p, tabId: 'trending')
```

Use a stable ID for the tab (an enum name or a key), not the index, if tabs can be reordered.

## Gotchas

- **Same grid twice in one tab:** if a product can appear in two sections of the same tab (e.g. "Recommended" and "All"), include the section in the tag too.
- **Don't wrap the whole card in the Hero** if it contains text or buttons. Use the image only, or add a `flightShuttleBuilder` with a `Material` ancestor to avoid text-style glitches mid-flight.
- **Same shape on both ends:** if the grid image has rounded corners and the detail image doesn't, add a `createRectTween` or a `flightShuttleBuilder` that animates `ClipRRect` radius.
- **Returning to a different tab:** the tag returns the Hero to the tab it came from, which is the correct behavior. Don't switch tabs while the route is open.
- **Nested navigators:** if each tab has its own `Navigator`, Heroes only fly within one navigator. Push the detail route on the root navigator (`Navigator.of(context, rootNavigator: true)`) if you want the transition to cover the bottom nav bar. Otherwise the default is fine.

If you tell me where the grid and tab code live, I can apply this directly to your files and add a widget test that checks that no duplicate tags exist across tabs.

